## Beyond the Viewport: Querying a Container

Media queries respond to the **viewport's** size, which works well for page-level layout decisions but falls short for genuinely reusable components: a card component might need to look different depending on how much space its *actual parent container* gives it — which could be a full-width page section, a narrow sidebar, or a small grid cell — entirely independent of the overall browser viewport size. **Container queries** solve exactly this by letting an element respond to the size of its nearest **queried ancestor**, not the viewport.

## Setting Up a Container

To make an element queryable, declare it as a containment context using `container-type`, optionally giving it a name with `container-name` (or combined via the `container` shorthand):

```css
.sidebar {
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: sidebar;
  /* shorthand: container: sidebar / inline-size; */
}
```

`container-type: inline-size` establishes size containment along the inline axis (width, in most writing modes), which is the most common choice for responsive component queries.

## Writing a Container Query

```css
.card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

@container sidebar (min-width: 400px) {
  .card {
    flex-direction: row;
  }
}
```

Any `.card` nested inside an element with `container-name: sidebar` switches from a stacked (column) to a side-by-side (row) layout once that specific container reaches 400px wide — completely independent of the overall page/viewport width. The same `.card` component, if placed inside a different, narrower container elsewhere on the page, would correctly remain stacked, since it's responding to its own immediate container's size, not a single global page breakpoint.

## Why This Matters for True Component Reusability

Before container queries, a component's internal responsive behavior could only be tied to the global viewport, which meant the *same* component often needed different variants or extra wrapper-specific override classes depending on where in the layout it was placed (full-width vs. sidebar vs. grid cell). Container queries let a single component definition correctly adapt to its actual available space everywhere it's used, which is a significant step toward genuinely self-contained, drop-in-anywhere components.

## Container Query Units

Alongside `@container`, new length units resolve relative to the queried container rather than the viewport: `cqw`/`cqh` (percentage of container width/height), `cqi`/`cqb` (percentage of container inline/block size), and `cqmin`/`cqmax`. These let values like `font-size` scale fluidly relative to the component's own container rather than the page.

codeExamples.code:
.card-wrapper {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.5rem;
}

@container (min-width: 350px) {
  .card {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
  }
}

.widget {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.widget h2 {
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 5cqi, 2rem);
}

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## gap: One Property for Spacing Between Items

The `gap` property (along with its longhands `row-gap` and `column-gap`) adds consistent spacing **between** the direct children of a Flexbox or Grid container, without adding any space before the first item or after the last. This directly solves a long-standing pain point that previously required applying margin to children and then separately zeroing it out on the first or last child (or using an adjacent-sibling selector trick) to avoid unwanted extra space at a layout's outer edges.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 1.5rem; /* shorthand for row-gap and column-gap both */
}

.row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem 2rem; /* row-gap: 1rem, column-gap: 2rem */
}
```

## Why gap Is Preferable to Margin for This Purpose

```css
/* Old approach: margin + manual cleanup */
.list-item {
  margin-right: 1rem;
}
.list-item:last-child {
  margin-right: 0;
}

/* Modern approach: a single declaration on the parent, no per-child exceptions */
.list {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

The `gap`-based approach is shorter, lives in one place (the container, not scattered across child selectors), and automatically handles wrapped rows in a `flex-wrap: wrap` layout correctly too — `gap` adds spacing both between items in the same row **and** between separate wrapped rows, consistently, which margin-based approaches handle much more awkwardly.

## gap's History: Grid First, Then Flexbox

`gap` was originally specified for CSS Grid only (initially even under the name `grid-gap`, now a deprecated alias for `gap`), and for several years was not supported in Flexbox contexts at all — developers had to use the margin-and-cleanup technique for flex layouts during that period. Browser support for `gap` in Flexbox arrived later (widely available in all major browsers since 2021), and projects needing to support noticeably older browser versions should verify `gap`-in-flexbox support before relying on it as the sole spacing mechanism, though at this point it is safe to use in essentially all modern production contexts.

## gap Also Works in Multi-column Layout

`gap` additionally applies to CSS multi-column layout (`column-count`/`columns`), controlling the space between columns of flowing text — a third context, alongside Grid and Flexbox, where the same single property name provides consistent spacing control.

codeExamples.code:
.tag-list {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
}

.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  gap: 12px;
}

.toolbar > button {
  padding: 8px 16px;
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
A **media query** applies styles based on the overall **browser viewport's** size (or other device characteristics), regardless of where in the page an element actually sits or how much space its specific parent container gives it.

A **container query** applies styles based on the size of a specific **ancestor container element** that has been explicitly marked as queryable, completely independent of the overall viewport size:

```css
.card-container {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card {
    flex-direction: row;
  }
}
```

The practical difference: a component styled with a media query always looks the same regardless of where it's placed on the page (it only cares about the whole viewport width), while a component styled with a container query can look different in different places on the *same* page at the *same* viewport width, depending on how much space its specific immediate container actually provides — for example, appearing compact in a narrow sidebar but spacious in a wide main content area, simultaneously, on the same page load. This makes container queries especially valuable for building genuinely reusable components that need to adapt to wherever they're dropped in a layout, not just to the page as a whole.

Before any `@container` rule can match, an ancestor element must be explicitly opted into being a **query container** using the `container-type` property (optionally paired with `container-name`, or combined via the `container` shorthand):

```css
.panel {
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: panel;
}

@container panel (min-width: 500px) {
  .panel-content {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }
}
```

- **`container-type: inline-size`** is the most common value — it establishes size containment along the inline axis (width, in standard horizontal writing modes), which is what allows descendants to query the container's width via `@container`. Setting this also applies CSS layout/size containment to the element itself, meaning its own size can no longer depend on its children's content in certain ways (the container's size must be independently determinable, since descendants are about to query it — this prevents circular sizing dependencies).
- **`container-name`** is optional; without it, any `@container` rule with no name specified queries the *nearest* ancestor that has `container-type` set, which works fine for simple cases but can become ambiguous in deeply nested containers, which is why naming containers explicitly is recommended for any non-trivial component library.

A key practical consequence of `container-type: inline-size` establishing containment: the queried container's own width can no longer be determined by the natural/intrinsic width of its own content (since that could create a dependency loop with the descendants querying it) — it must get its width from somewhere else in the layout, such as a parent's `display: grid`/`flex` sizing, a percentage width, or an explicit value.
